<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shiba clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shiba clan was a Japanese warrior family, a branch of the Ashikaga, which held hereditary office as one of the three deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>, or kanrei, of the Muromachi shogunate. Its heads were military governors of Echizen, Owari and Tōtōmi, but a succession dispute in the mid-15th century fed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a>, and their retainers, the Asakura and the Oda, took over their lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shiba-clan.jpg" alt="Shiba clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashikaga_mon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Shiba descended from Ashikaga Yasuuji and from the Seiwa-Genji. The clan name dates from the end of the 13th century and from Shiba Ieuji, a son of Yasuuji. The family was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu Province</a>, in the north of Honshū, and also inherited the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period it was treated as a cadet branch of the Ashikaga clan and used the Ashikaga name; Shiba became its family name only in the Muromachi period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shiba Takatsune (1305–1367) raised the clan's standing when he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> (1305–1358) in the fighting against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> in 1335. Once the Ashikaga shogunate was established, Takauji made him shugo governor of Echizen and Wakasa provinces. His son Shiba Yoshimasa (1350–1410) held the office of kanrei, deputy of the shogun, from 1379 to 1397, and the office was held after him by his son Shiba Yoshishige (1371–1418) and his grandson Shiba Yoshiatsu (1397–1434).</p><p>By the mid-15th century the Shiba were divided by factional dispute. The feud within the clan and with other clans was one cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> (1467–1477), and the Shiba were unable to make the transition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> daimyō. They lost Echizen to the Asakura in the 1470s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-toshikage">Asakura Toshikage</a> took their power by usurpation.</p><p>By 1550 the clan was represented by Shiba Yoshimune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, whose seat was Kiyosu Castle. He was a figurehead behind whom the Yamato no kami branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a> ruled. When Oda Nobutomo killed Yoshimune in 1554, the clan effectively came to an end, although his son Shiba Yoshikane lived until 1572.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shiba clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Asakura Toshikage, a retainer of the <b>Shiba</b> governors of Echizen, used the Onin War to take control of the province, seizing power from his masters around 1471.</blockquote><a href="/state/asakura-clan-domain">Asakura clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
